// =============================================================================
// Lint waivers, scoped as tightly as possible.
//
// rtl/picorv32.v is vendored and must not be modified (see CLAUDE.md), so its
// pre-existing style warnings can't be fixed at the source. Every waiver
// below is filed to that one path only - it has zero effect on any file we
// own, so a new warning in our own RTL still fails lint.
//
//   BLKSEQ        - picorv32's core FSM uses blocking assignments ('=') in
//                   posedge blocks throughout; that's the vendored coding
//                   style, not something to rewrite.
//   DECLFILENAME  - the file defines picorv32_regs/_axi/_wb alongside
//                   picorv32, so the filename can't match every module name.
//   GENUNNAMED    - unnamed generate blocks (ENABLE_FAST_MUL/ENABLE_MUL/
//                   ENABLE_DIV branches) from the upstream source.
//   MULTITOP      - same multi-module-per-file situation as DECLFILENAME;
//                   we only ever instantiate `picorv32`, never the others.
//   UNUSEDSIGNAL  - unused debug/trace signals (dbg_*, waddr[5]/raddr*[5]
//                   from the register file) internal to the core, not
//                   reachable or meaningful from soc_top.v.
//
// Deliberately NOT waived: the SYNCASYNCNET warning at the soc_top.v /
// picorv32.v boundary (rst_n used async in soc_top.v, sync inside
// picorv32.v) - that one crosses into code we own and was flagged for
// human review rather than suppressed here.
// =============================================================================
`verilator_config

lint_off -rule BLKSEQ       -file "rtl/picorv32.v"
lint_off -rule DECLFILENAME -file "rtl/picorv32.v"
lint_off -rule GENUNNAMED   -file "rtl/picorv32.v"
lint_off -rule MULTITOP     -file "rtl/picorv32.v"
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "rtl/picorv32.v"
